12:25:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand that feeling. It's genuinely jarring when a “fact” that everyone knows, that your teacher taught you, that’s baked into jokes and insults across languages, turns out to be false. It feels like the mental floorboards are being taken out from under your feet. That discomfort you're feeling is completely normal, and it's called **cognitive dissonance**—the friction between a deeply held belief and new, contradictory information. So, let’s talk about why “common sense” feels so true and why it can still be so wrong. ### What “Common Sense” Actually Is “Common sense” isn’t truth; it’s just a set of assumptions that are common in a particular culture at a particular time. A century ago, it was “just common sense” that heavier objects fall faster than light ones. It was common sense that the sun orbited the earth—you could see it with your own eyes. It was common sense that stomach ulcers were caused by spicy food and stress, not by bacteria. In each case, the science felt “weird” because it asked people to discard something they’d believed with absolute certainty. The goldfish myth is in exactly that category. The “weird” feeling you have isn't a sign that the science is flimsy; it's a sign that you're human, and you've been marinating in a cultural belief for a very long time. ### The Problem of “Just Watching” That “common sense” usually comes from casual, unscientific observation. You watch a goldfish and it *seems* to swim in circles without a care. You don’t see its memory because memory is internal. You can’t see the chemical changes in its brain when it learns a feeding routine. You can’t see it recognizing you unless you’re trained to look for subtle cues, like a change in swimming speed or fin position. Science exists precisely because common sense and naked-eye observation are so often wrong. The lab experiments aren’t “a few tricks”; they’re a systematic way of asking a question that avoids our human biases. They don't make goldfish smart; they just *reveal* the intelligence that was always there, hidden behind a poker face and a fishbowl. ### Trusting the Process, Not the Proverb Here’s a helpful way to reframe this. You’re not being asked to “throw out” common sense based on blind faith in a lab. You’re being shown that what you thought was common sense was actually a piece of cultural folklore that was never scientifically true in the first place. The saying “memory of a goldfish” is the real outlier, not the decades of replicated research. When we know better, we change the saying. We used to say someone was “deaf as a post” or “dumb as a doornail,” and now we recognize that’s just a poetic way of expressing frustration, not biology. The goldfish idiom is the same: a wonderfully vivid phrase for a forgetful person, built on a completely mistaken idea about a fish. That weird feeling you have right now? That’s your common sense updating itself in real time. It’s uncomfortable, but it’s also the hallmark of critical thinking. You’re now in a very small club: people who know the actual truth about goldfish, and who understand the much more fascinating, and much more common, story of how a myth can fool the entire world.
