h the hierarchy every three seconds. ### 2. The Myth Predicts Things That Simply Don't Happen If a pet goldfish actually had a shockingly short memory, its behavior would be bizarrely broken, and any casual owner would notice. It would: - Be constantly discovering its own reflection and reacting as if to a new fish. - Forget the location of food the instant it drifted away, never being able to find flakes on the water's surface. - Be completely indifferent to the glass walls, constantly bumping its nose in a state of perpetual surprise. - Be unable to recognize a net from one day to the next, making it trivially easy to catch every single time without any chase at all (which contradicts the earlier net discussion). None of these things happen, even with the most neglected, untrained pet goldfish. The fact that a goldfish even *seems* to know where to look for food, and can be hard to catch after a few attempts, is proof it's doing exactly what the lab fish are doing—just more subtly. ### 3. The Lab Just Makes the Invisible Visible The purpose of the lab studies isn't to inject memory into a fish that lacks it, like installing a computer chip. The purpose is to design a controlled, unambiguous situation where the fish can "tell" us what it remembers, by pressing a lever or choosing a door. In a home tank, the memories are still there, but the fish can't press a lever to declare them. Instead, you see it as a fish that swims to the front glass at 5:30 PM and waits. That's not a reflex; it's a silent, self-taught lever press. Your concern is that a normal, untrained goldfish might somehow slip back into a "short-memory-like" state of being. But neurons don't work that way. Memory isn't a muscle that atrophies from lack of training; it's a fundamental property of how the brain processes experience. An untrained goldfish isn't an amnesiac; it's just a goldfish whose memories are mundane. It remembers its tank mates, its tank layout, its feeding times, and its owner, because its survival and well-being—even in a living room—depend on those memories. So, you're absolutely right that the lab fish get intensive training, but that training just gives them an unusually interesting set of things to remember and a way to show off. The ordinary pet goldfish is still building a rich, long-lasting mental model of its world every day, entirely on its own. The myth didn't describe the untrained fish; it just completely misunderstood everything about the species.
