t to re-check every single question year after year. The goldfish myth, once baked into the database, becomes immortal. ### 3. The Myth is "Catchy," Which is Exactly What Trivia Wants Trivia games thrive on surprising, counterintuitive facts that make people say "Wow, really?" The three-second memory is a perfect trivia fact: it's short, shocking, and makes you look at a common animal differently. The correct version—"Goldfish have good long-term memory for weeks or months"—is also surprising, but it's a correction, not the original myth. Trivia editors, consciously or not, prefer the punchier, older factoid because it has a proven record of engaging users. The truth is less sticky than the myth, which is exactly why the myth survives in entertainment contexts. ### 4. The "Human 10% Brain" Parallel This isn't unique to goldfish. The "humans only use 10% of their brains" myth is thoroughly debunked. Yet trivia apps and quiz games still sometimes list it as true, or ask "What percentage of the brain do humans use?" with 10% as the accepted answer. The myth is so culturally dominant that even fact-checkers who should know better sometimes slip, because they're human and they trust their own memory over an obscure correction. The goldfish myth is in exactly that category: a super-myth that has overwhelmed the corrective efforts. ### 5. User Feedback Can Actually Reinforce the Myth When a trivia app does get corrected by an expert user, the developers face a dilemma. They might receive 99 angry emails from users saying "Your quiz is wrong, everyone knows goldfish have short memories!" and one email from a fish biologist with a citation. Faced with a flood of user complaints, the developer might revert the question to the "popular" answer, or simply delete it to avoid controversy. The economic incentive is to please the crowd, not to uphold peer-reviewed truth. So, you're absolutely right to be baffled. It’s astonishing that in an age of information, a completely false fact can be sold as "fact-checked." But it’s a testament to the myth's incredible cultural power—a power that has nothing to do with goldfish and everything to do with the human appetite for a good story and the flawed, messy, circular way entertainment media verifies the world. The trivia app isn’t a final authority. It’s just the myth's latest, slickest disguise.
